  {
   "turn": 8,
   "response_text": "Based on my analysis, the training appears to be **working as designed**, not showing any configuration faults. Here's my assessment:\n\n**Why this is not an incident:**\n\n1. **High label noise (33%) is intentional:** The config explicitly sets `label_noise_fraction: 0.33`. This is a controlled experiment to test model robustness to noisy labels. With one-third of training labels corrupted, poor convergence and low accuracy are expected.\n\n2. **Normal training curve for noisy labels:** \n   - Training loss decreases steadily but slowly (0.667 \u2192 0.653)\n   - Validation accuracy plateaus early (~84%) \n   - Both behaviors are exactly what we'd expect when training on 33% corrupted labels\n\n3. **All hyperparameters are reasonable:**\n   - LR = 0.01 (standard for Adam)\n   - Batch size = 256 (reasonable for this dataset)\n   - Weight decay = 0.0001 (small regularization, appropriate)\n   - Adam optimizer with BCEWithLogitsLoss (correct for binary classification)\n\n4. **No errors in logs:** The training completed successfully with exit code 0, no exceptions or warnings.\n\n5. **Consistent implementation:** The label noise is applied deterministically using `nested_prefix_indices()` which ensures reproducibility and consistency across runs.\n\nThis is a **healthy run** demonstrating robust training on corrupted data. The slow convergence and plateau are expected symptoms of the high label noise rate, not a fault.",
